|~ `::excerpt`, the kind of a value told within a room of characters,
   and what is declared under it: the names it wires, the kind of the
   value told, its count and the parts it shows, and its laws.
   The rules it holds: [QSPRN#excerpt], [QSERR#alert]. ~|

:excerpt
  |~~ An excerpt tells a value too large for its room: the kind of the
      value, how many parts it has and the parts that fit whole, taken from
      its two ends inward, `::excerpt{:of ::vec :count 3410 :parts {:"0" 0
      :"1" 1 :"3409" 3409}}`. A vector, a set, a quote and a doc show their
      elements under their indices and a map its entries under their keys,
      while a string and a value with no parts or one its kind prints show
      the first and the last characters of their text under their offsets,
      which `drop` reads to them. `excerpt` answers one, and a host prints
      a fact of a failure past the room of a fact so, and an answer past
      the budget of every answer within its refusal.

      An excerpt is a value of its own kind, read by its names: `count`
      answers the count of the value told, and no verb of that value
      answers it, so a step after it meets an excerpt and never data with
      a hole. The whole is read live, a fact of a failure by `!|` and an
      answer past the budget by `answer` of the cell that keeps it.
      ::link~(::any/excerpt | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ The kind of the value told, its count and the parts it shows. ~~| ::map
    | wires :of |~~ The tag of the value told, which `tag` answers of it. ~~| ::tag
    | wires :count
        |~~ The elements of the value told, its entries or the characters of
            its text, as `count` counts them.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | wires :parts
        |~~ The parts the excerpt shows whole, under their indices, their
            keys or the offsets of their characters.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ An excerpt is the map of the kind of the value it tells, its
            count and the parts it shows, which `excerpt` writes,
            `x | excerpt`.
        ~~|
    | proves :countsTheValueItTells
        |~~ `count` answers the count of the value told. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | count | eq 3410)
    | proves :showsTheEndsOfAVector
        |~~ The first and the last elements show under their indices. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | parts | get [/0 /3409] | eq [0 3409])
    | proves :answersNoVerbOfTheValueItTells
        |~~ An excerpt is no vector: a verb of vectors refuses it. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | take 3 !| tag | eq ::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError)
    | proves :readsBackAsItself
        |~~ An excerpt prints and reads back as itself. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | :e it | print | parse | open | eq e)
